tately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n  1451→      ],\n  1452→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  1453→      \"ipa\": null,\n  1454→      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n  1455→      \"audio_url\": null,\n  1456→      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n  1457→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n  1458→    }\n  1459→  ],\n  1460→  \"glossary\": null,\n  1461→  \"overview\": {\n  1462→    \"structure\": {\n  1463→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1464→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1465→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1466→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1467→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1468→    },\n  1469→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1470→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the grip of a merciless plague known as the Red Death, which sweeps across the land with terrifying efficiency—causing agonizing pain, violent bleeding, and death marked by scarlet stains. Amid the chaos and despair, Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and defiant spirit, chooses to escape the suffering by retreating with a thousand courtiers into the isolated, fortified abbey he has transformed into a sanctuary of pleasure and excess. Sealed off from the outside world with iron gates and heated furnaces, the abbey becomes a fortress of illusion, where the horrors beyond are forgotten in favor of opulence and revelry. The heart of this masquerade is a grand masked ball, held in seven lavishly decorated chambers, each illuminated by a different hue, culminating in a chilling black chamber lit by blood-red stained glass and dominated by a massive ebony clock that chimes each hour, momentarily silencing the festivities and reminding all of the passage of time and the inevitability of death.\\n\\nAs the night unfolds, the revelers indulge in dance and spectacle, their masks and costumes serving as both disguise and denial of reality. Yet, the oppressive presence of the ebony clock and the growing unease it inspires begin to erode their sense of security. At midnight, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in funeral shrouds and stained with blood, moving with unnatural calm through the chambers. No one dares stop him, and his presence grows more menacing with each step. Prince Prospero, consumed by rage and disbelief, confronts the figure with a dagger, only to collapse dead upon reaching the black room. In that moment, the truth is revealed: the figure is not a guest, but the plague incarnate, having infiltrated the abbey through the very isolation it was meant to prevent. The clock stops, darkness descends, and one by one, every reveler succumbs to the Red Death, their lives extinguished in the same manner as those outside.\\n\\nThe narrative concludes with the complete annihilation of the revelers, underscoring the futility of attempting to escape mortality through wealth, power, or denial. Prince Prospero’s arrogance in believing he could outwit death by sequestering himself and indulging in pleasure is his ultimate downfall. His relationship with his courtiers—built on privilege and spectacle—collapses under the weight of shared fate. The turning point comes not with a battle or revelation, but with the silent, inevitable arrival of the Red Death, which penetrates even the most fortified sanctuary. The story ends not with triumph, but with the grim realization that death is universal, indifferent, and inescapable, rendering all earthly defenses meaningless.\",\n  1471→      \"themes\": [\n  1472→        \"identity\",\n  1473→        \"ambition\",\n  1474→        \"loss\"\n  1475→      ],\n  1476→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1477→    },\n  1478→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1479→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1480→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1481→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1