'Red Death,' a horrific plague marked by sharp pains, profuse bleeding, and scarlet stains that isolate victims from society. Prince Prospero, in defiance of the plague, retreats with a thousand courtiers to his secluded, castellated abbey, sealing all entrances with iron gates and furnaces to ensure total isolation. Inside, he hosts a lavish masked ball in seven uniquely colored chambers, each lit by blood-red firelight streaming through stained-glass windows, culminating in a black room with scarlet panes where a massive ebony clock chimes hourly, halting the revelry and instilling dread. As midnight strikes, a mysterious figure dressed as the Red Death—cloaked in grave-habits and drenched in blood—enters unchallenged, moving slowly through the chambers until confronting Prince Prospero, who, enraged, pursues him with a dagger but collapses dead upon reaching the black room; the figure is revealed to be the plague itself, and one by one, all revelers die as the clock stops and darkness claims the abbey.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Prince Prospero</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">The masked figure (Red Death)</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Courtiers</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Musicians</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Buffoons</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Improvisatori</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Ballet-dancers</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Beauty</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n           